---
import RocmGuide from '../../layouts/RocmGuide.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Cite from '../../components/rocm/Cite.astro';
import GoDeeper from '../../components/rocm/GoDeeper.astro';
import Sources from '../../components/rocm/Sources.astro';
import type { CiteKey } from '../../lib/rocm-guide';

const used = [
  'rocm101Notes',
  'rocm10Matrix',
  'linuxReqs',
  'productR9700',
  'hsaOverride2353',
  'rocr',
] as const satisfies readonly CiteKey[];

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'rdna-vs-cdna', text: 'RDNA vs CDNA' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'ids', text: 'gfx IDs you will actually type' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'why', text: 'Why the gfx target matters' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'override', text: 'HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION' },
];
---

<RocmGuide page="gfx" headings={headings}>
  <p>
    Every AMD GPU this site talks about has two names. The sticker name is for buying
    (<a href="https://www.amd.com/en/products/graphics/workstations/radeon-ai-pro/ai-9000-series/amd-radeon-ai-pro-r9700.html">Radeon AI PRO R9700</a>).<Cite id="productR9700" used={used} />
    The <strong>gfx ID</strong> is the LLVM target the compiler and the wheels care about. On
    ROCm 10.1.0’s hardware table the R9700 is <code>gfx1201</code>, architecture <strong>RDNA 4</strong>.<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The living card list — VRAM, official vs unofficial, and per-SKU override notes — is the
    <a href="/gpus">GPU directory</a>. This page explains the IDs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rdna-vs-cdna">RDNA vs CDNA</h2>
  <p>
    AMD ships two compute-relevant GPU families in current ROCm notes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>RDNA</strong> — the Radeon (and Ryzen iGPU) line. Consumer and workstation cards you
      can actually buy for a home lab. The 10.1.0 table labels R9700 / RX 9070-class as RDNA 4
      (<code>gfx1201</code>), RX 7900-class as RDNA 3 (<code>gfx1100</code> / <code>1101</code> /
      <code>1102</code>), and older PRO parts such as W6800 as RDNA 2 (<code>gfx1030</code>).<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CDNA</strong> — the Instinct datacenter line. Same ROCm docs, different SKUs, often
      different OS/kernel footnotes. Do not copy an Instinct install row onto a Radeon and assume
      the kernels match.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Wavefront width and matrix-core details differ across those families. The 10.1.0 notes even
    call out rocSPARSE work that is wave32-aware on RDNA 4 (<code>gfx1201</code>) and leaves
    wave64 (CDNA) behavior unchanged.<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} />
    You do not need to memorize wave sizes. You do need to install the package suffix that matches
    your family — <code>gfx1201</code> for an R9700, <code>gfx110x</code> for a 7900 — as the
    <a href="/tutorials/install-rocm">install tutorial</a> describes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ids">gfx IDs you will actually type</h2>
  <p>
    From the ROCm 10.1.0 device table (Radeon rows), plus this site’s directory. Re-read the
    <a href="https://rocm.docs.amd.com/en/docs-10.1.0/about/release-notes.html">10.1.0 notes</a>
    and the <a href="https://rocm.docs.amd.com/en/docs-10.1.0/compatibility/compatibility-matrix.html">compatibility matrix</a>
    on install day.<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} /><Cite id="rocm10Matrix" used={used} />
  </p>
  <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="table-lab min-w-[36rem]">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>gfx</th>
          <th>Arch</th>
          <th>Example SKUs on the 10.1 table</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>gfx1201</code></td>
          <td>RDNA 4</td>
          <td>Radeon AI PRO R9700 / R9700S / R9600D / R9600; RX 9070 XT / GRE / 9070</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>gfx1200</code></td>
          <td>RDNA 4</td>
          <td>RX 9060 XT (and XT LP), RX 9060, RX 9050</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>gfx1100</code></td>
          <td>RDNA 3</td>
          <td>RX 7900 XTX / XT / GRE; W7900; W7800</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>gfx1101</code></td>
          <td>RDNA 3</td>
          <td>RX 7800 XT / 7700 XT / 7700; W7700; PRO V710</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>gfx1102</code></td>
          <td>RDNA 3</td>
          <td>RX 7600 XT / 7600</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>gfx1030</code></td>
          <td>RDNA 2</td>
          <td>PRO W6800; PRO V620. Consumer 6950 XT is <em>not</em> the same official row — see the directory.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Ryzen AI iGPUs have their own gfx IDs (<code>gfx1150</code>, <code>gfx1151</code>,
    <code>gfx1103</code>, …) on the same 10.1.0 table.<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} />
    The cited Ryzen AI Max+ 395 / Strix Halo (Radeon 8060S, <code>gfx1151</code>) guide is
    <a href="/gpus/strix-halo">/gpus/strix-halo</a>.
    Radeon 780M / 760M (<code>gfx1103</code>) and 880M / 890M (<code>gfx1150</code>) live on
    <a href="/gpus/ryzen-igpu">/gpus/ryzen-igpu</a>.
    If <code>llama-cli --list-devices</code> shows an iGPU next to a discrete R9700, pick the
    discrete device. The directory does not list every APU; it lists the cards a home lab actually
    shops.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why the gfx target matters</h2>
  <p>
    A HIP binary, a PyTorch wheel, and a vLLM image each ship GPU code objects for a
    <em>list</em> of targets. If your card’s gfx is not on that list, the runtime has no kernel
    to launch. Typical symptoms: <code>hipErrorNoBinaryForGpu</code>, a library looking for
    tensiles in the wrong directory, or a silent CPU fallback (VRAM meter never moves).
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why this site’s llama.cpp HIP configure line sets <code>AMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx1201</code>
    on an R9700, and why “I installed ROCm” is not the same as “this wheel lists my ISA.” Official
    support is the matrix / notes row.<Cite id="rocm10Matrix" used={used} /><Cite id="linuxReqs" used={used} />
    Community “it ran” is something else. We label them separately.
  </p>

  <h2 id="override">HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION</h2>
  <p>
    <code>HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION</code> is an unofficial environment variable some people set so
    a process will load code objects built for a <em>nearby</em> gfx. The usual example in ROCm
    issue threads is <code>10.3.0</code> so a <code>gfx1031</code> card (RX 6700-class) uses
    <code>gfx1030</code> objects.<Cite id="hsaOverride2353" used={used} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Format in that community usage is <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code> (for example
    <code>10.3.0</code> ↔ gfx1030). It applies to the process — it is not a per-GPU switch on a
    mixed machine.
  </p>

  <Callout type="warn" title="When it helps, and the risks">
    <p>
      <strong>When people try it:</strong> a build or wheel lists a close relative (gfx1030) and
      not your exact ID (gfx1031), and Vulkan is not an option for that app.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Risks:</strong> you are running ISA that was not validated on your chip. Wrong
      instruction selection, memory faults, and “it works until it does not” are all on the table.
      The GitHub thread is a community workaround, not an AMD how-to.<Cite id="hsaOverride2353" used={used} />
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>On current gfx1201 + ROCm 10.x:</strong> you should not need it. The R9700 and RX
      9070 XT are first-class <code>gfx1201</code> rows. If HIP fails there, fix the target list
      or use Vulkan llama.cpp — do not spray an override. That is this site’s directory note, not
      an AMD quote.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <GoDeeper title="Is this in the official environment-variable list?">
    <p>
      AMD’s ROCR docs have an environment-variables reference.<Cite id="rocr" used={used} />
      The specific path we tried
      (<code>…/ROCR-Runtime/en/latest/reference/environment-variables.html</code>) returned 404
      on 2026-10-12. We do <strong>not</strong> claim HSA_OVERRIDE is an official, documented
      ROCr knob. Until an official page lists it, treat it as community-only.
    </p>
    <p>
      <!-- not recorded: record the live official env-var URL if/when it lists HSA_OVERRIDE_GFX_VERSION. -->
      Other <code>HSA_*</code> names exist for WSL2 detection in the 10.1.0 notes
      (<code>HSA_ENABLE_DXG_DETECTION</code>).<Cite id="rocm101Notes" used={used} />
      That is a different variable.
    </p>
  </GoDeeper>

  <p>
    Card-by-card override notes: <a href="/gpus">GPU directory</a>. Tracker rows:
    <a href="/rocm-problems/hsa-override-unofficial">HSA_OVERRIDE unofficial</a>,
    <a href="/rocm-problems/gfx1031-hsa-override">gfx1031</a>. If a log looks like a gfx
    mismatch, paste it in the <a href="/troubleshoot/errors">error matcher</a>.
  </p>

  <Sources used={used} />
</RocmGuide>
